th ours and there's a manhole  cover a thing in there that was barely  there in the end it was irritating they  go over the manhole covers of cooking  the car rolled over the brakes squeak  was added and I think the car comes in  is actually a Plymouth and we actually  went and recorded that from a  collector's car the usual thing the  battery went flat as soon as we went to  start it the sound we're not pushing it  down the road at Skywalker to get it  started  the tinging fan was something that gary  particularly liked rather than the clean  fan or you know particularly period fan  just wanted that thing sound and always  greater Foley details of the coin and  it's a very delicate stuff and the the  couple arguing it across the hall in the  New York apartment and then the violin  scale was being played and people  walking up stairs with hippie but  muffled feet all that stuff all sort of  gives it that feel of New York  excellent I'm gonna go to max mark Greg  can you talk about your respective roles  on the film and how you work together  much  I'm the Mad Max equivalent of Richard  Holmes I'm the supervising sound editor  and I'm also here with my co nominee  David White who is our sound designer  he's here in the front row and my job is  to record edit design all the sounds  that you will hear much like Richard  just talked about all these lovely  little D  tales that create a scene and then  provide them to Gregg to mix them to  create a finished and fleshed out proper  soundtrack what were the particular  challenges of Mad Max I mean as you can  imagine this is an incredibly dense film  sonically as well as visually so the  challenge was this year enormity and  capturing and editing that much sound  but I think our real success and Gregg  will speak to this in more detail was in  a movie that is arguably going to be a  loud movie how do we control that much  sound so that there's always focus and  there's always a narrative thread with  sound because the film